# TV power scheduling over the LAN, via the Roku ECP HTTP API. No CEC, no extra
# hardware — the Wyse is DisplayPort-only and DP carries no CEC line anyway.
#
# Install with:  crontab -u wall deploy/tv-power.crontab
# Replace TV_IP with the television's reserved address before installing.
#
# This turns the *display* on and off. The Wyse itself never sleeps; see
# deploy/kiosk.sh for the xset calls that keep it awake.
#
# The TV needs all of these set or this silently does nothing:
#   Settings > System > Advanced > Control by mobile apps = Enabled (not Limited)
#   Fast TV Start = On          keeps the network stack alive while "off"
#   Power > Auto power savings = Off   or it kills the display after ~4h idle
#   Screen saver = disabled
#   ...and a DHCP reservation, or TV_IP moves and this stops working quietly.

TV_IP=192.168.1.x

# Put the dashboard back on screen before the TV comes on. The dashboard shares
# a session with a usable desktop, so if the browser was closed to do something
# else and never reopened, the wall would show the XFCE desktop all day. This is
# a no-op when it is already running.
55 5 * * * DISPLAY=:0 /opt/wall-dashboard/deploy/ensure-kiosk.sh >/dev/null 2>&1

# On at 06:00, then select the input the Wyse is plugged into. The sleep is
# there because PowerOn returns before the TV can accept an input change.
0 6 * * * curl -sf -m 10 -d '' "http://$TV_IP:8060/keypress/PowerOn" && sleep 5 && curl -sf -m 10 -d '' "http://$TV_IP:8060/launch/tvinput.hdmi1"

# Off at 22:00.
0 22 * * * curl -sf -m 10 -d '' "http://$TV_IP:8060/keypress/PowerOff"
